1
00:00:04,442 --> 00:00:16,869
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's the Susuma Sutta in the Sāṃyutta Nikaya, the Nidāna Sāṃyutta, Sutta number 70.

2
00:00:17,949 --> 00:00:21,091
[SPEAKER_01]: So, on that occasion, this is really the more important bit.

3
00:00:21,351 --> 00:00:33,398
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now, on that occasion, many bhikkhus had, in the presence of the Buddha, described aññā, which is like, seems to be the, kind of, the

4
00:00:34,309 --> 00:00:38,570
[SPEAKER_01]: Full knowledge or complete knowledge of the arahant.

5
00:00:41,190 --> 00:00:43,191
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's defined as this.

6
00:00:43,231 --> 00:00:48,472
[SPEAKER_01]: So, we understand birth is destroyed, the holy life has been lived, what should be done has been done.

7
00:00:48,992 --> 00:00:50,492
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no more of this.

8
00:00:55,473 --> 00:01:02,134
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the Venerable Sussamo has heard, hears that apparently lots of bhikkhus have described this knowledge.

9
00:01:03,348 --> 00:01:18,511
[SPEAKER_01]: In the presence of the Buddha, and then he approaches venerable... sorry, venerable sister approaches these these bhikkhus and asks those bhikkhus, is it really true that in the presence of the Buddha, the complete knowledge has been described?

10
00:01:19,091 --> 00:01:23,272
[SPEAKER_01]: The complete knowledge, full knowledge, has been described by the venerables.

11
00:01:23,792 --> 00:01:29,053
[SPEAKER_01]: We understand birth is destroyed, the holy life has been lived, what should be done has been done.

12
00:01:29,853 --> 00:01:30,714
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no more of this.

13
00:01:31,834 --> 00:01:32,294
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, friend?

14
00:01:34,841 --> 00:01:46,671
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, the system says, okay, then, so, knowing in this way, seeing in this way, do you, venerables, experience various sorts of special powers?

15
00:01:46,691 --> 00:01:58,340
[SPEAKER_01]: Like... being many, you become one, being one, you become many, you walk on water, you walk through walls, you... you fly cross-legged through space.

16
00:01:58,860 --> 00:01:59,281
[SPEAKER_01]: No front.

17
00:02:01,942 --> 00:02:11,025
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, knowing in this way, seeing in this way, do you venerables dwell, having contacted with the body, those peaceful immaterial liberations that transcend matter?

18
00:02:12,126 --> 00:02:12,546
[SPEAKER_01]: No, friend.

19
00:02:14,047 --> 00:02:21,869
[SPEAKER_01]: Here now, venerables, sirs, this answer and the non-attainment of these beings, these things, friends, how could this be?

20
00:02:24,370 --> 00:02:26,111
[SPEAKER_00]: How could they be declaring the final knowledge?

21
00:02:26,831 --> 00:02:27,031
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

22
00:02:27,231 --> 00:02:28,472
[SPEAKER_00]: Without any of those attainments?

23
00:02:28,552 --> 00:02:28,752
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

24
00:02:34,376 --> 00:02:36,556
[SPEAKER_01]: We are liberated by understanding, friend.

25
00:02:39,397 --> 00:02:40,597
[SPEAKER_01]: Paññāvimutta.

26
00:02:44,378 --> 00:02:45,959
[SPEAKER_01]: We are liberated by understanding, friend.

27
00:02:46,279 --> 00:02:51,440
[SPEAKER_01]: I do not understand in detail the meaning of what has been said in brief by these venerable ones.

28
00:02:52,100 --> 00:03:00,722
[SPEAKER_01]: It would be good if the venerable ones could speak the truth so that I might understand in detail the meaning of what has been said in brief by the venerable ones.

29
00:03:02,253 --> 00:03:06,175
[SPEAKER_01]: You might understand friend Susumma, they say, or you might not understand.

30
00:03:06,755 --> 00:03:09,037
[SPEAKER_01]: Even so, we are liberated by understanding.

31
00:03:12,258 --> 00:03:21,924
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, Venerable Susumma, having got up from his seat, went to the Buddha, and paid respects, sat down on one side, and told... ...reported all that.

32
00:03:21,984 --> 00:03:22,844
[SPEAKER_01]: ...reported all that.

33
00:03:25,325 --> 00:03:30,048
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha says this, beforehand Susumma, this is the sentence, beforehand Susumma,

34
00:03:33,705 --> 00:03:37,867
[SPEAKER_01]: dhamma, citti, niññāna, is the Pali word.

35
00:03:40,369 --> 00:03:45,732
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe we read first the sentence, and then we can talk about the individual Pali word.

36
00:03:46,412 --> 00:03:46,712
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

37
00:03:47,173 --> 00:03:48,073
[SPEAKER_01]: What does the Buddha say?

38
00:03:49,574 --> 00:03:51,135
[SPEAKER_01]: Before... well, okay, so I'll...

39
00:03:51,175 --> 00:03:51,335
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll...

40
00:03:51,495 --> 00:03:55,898
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll... my translation is, before Hansusama, the knowledge of the endurance of things.

41
00:03:56,978 --> 00:03:59,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Afterwards, the knowledge of nibbāna.

42
00:04:00,981 --> 00:04:03,222
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, then, the usual translations

43
00:04:04,604 --> 00:04:10,968
[SPEAKER_01]: I've had a look at various different sort of scholars, Pali scholars have translated it.

44
00:04:11,408 --> 00:04:13,369
[SPEAKER_01]: The knowledge of the stability of dhamma.

45
00:04:13,389 --> 00:04:16,331
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's dhamma... dhamma titiññāna.

46
00:04:16,791 --> 00:04:18,352
[SPEAKER_01]: So, ñāna is like the knowledge.

47
00:04:19,082 --> 00:04:21,403
[SPEAKER_01]: Stability is like standing or persisting.

48
00:04:21,423 --> 00:04:23,204
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like the third characteristic, isn't it?

49
00:04:23,605 --> 00:04:27,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Arising is manifest, ceasing is manifest, persisting while changing is manifest.

50
00:04:27,727 --> 00:04:28,848
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I think it is.

51
00:04:28,868 --> 00:04:40,115
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the normal translations are the knowledge of the stability of the dhamma, the knowledge of the regularity of the dhamma, the knowledge of the steadfast of the dhamma, the knowledge of the persistence of principles, the knowledge of the stability of natural principles.

52
00:04:40,695 --> 00:04:41,535
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, okay.

53
00:04:41,916 --> 00:04:43,497
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is all... it seems to be okay.

54
00:04:43,557 --> 00:04:45,658
[SPEAKER_00]: It's okay, but it's not as something they would...

55
00:04:46,078 --> 00:04:55,300
[SPEAKER_00]: It shouldn't be so directly pertaining to the understanding and being an arahant, which the Buddha clearly answered that it should pertain.

56
00:04:57,161 --> 00:04:59,962
[SPEAKER_00]: He asked, how are these people arahants without any of those powers?

57
00:05:00,022 --> 00:05:02,542
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha said, well, this is because this is what they know.

58
00:05:03,743 --> 00:05:11,605
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you take that to mean just the steadfastness of the dhamma, many people know that.

59
00:05:13,179 --> 00:05:14,220
[SPEAKER_00]: Many people can accept that.

60
00:05:14,240 --> 00:05:15,140
[SPEAKER_00]: Many people have faith in it.

61
00:05:15,160 --> 00:05:20,042
[SPEAKER_00]: Many people even understand that, to a great degree, but far from being anywhere nearly.

62
00:05:21,183 --> 00:05:21,723
[SPEAKER_00]: An arahant.

63
00:05:25,385 --> 00:05:30,647
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, you could look for something more concise, which is, I think, what you've done in that translation.

64
00:05:31,488 --> 00:05:35,590
[SPEAKER_00]: So, instead of dhammatitya, instead of dhamma meaning the dhamma, dhamma means phenomena.

65
00:05:36,308 --> 00:05:41,652
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they know the arising, the manifestation, persistence of a reasoned phenomenon.

66
00:05:41,672 --> 00:05:42,192
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

67
00:05:42,252 --> 00:05:45,595
[SPEAKER_00]: That then brings nibbāna to development, as in dispassion.

68
00:05:46,375 --> 00:05:54,521
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you understand the nature of anything that has appeared, the result of that understanding is always dispassion.

69
00:05:54,681 --> 00:05:56,122
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot result in other things.

70
00:05:57,363 --> 00:06:04,048
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you don't want to be dispassionate, if you're understanding things for what they are, how they persist, how they have arisen in that manner,

71
00:06:05,685 --> 00:06:09,368
[SPEAKER_00]: You will experience this passion towards the things, letting go, and so on.

72
00:06:10,048 --> 00:06:11,949
[SPEAKER_00]: So, nibbāna will be that automatic result.

73
00:06:13,230 --> 00:06:13,770
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's...

74
00:06:14,491 --> 00:06:18,433
[SPEAKER_01]: I think that's the interesting thing here.

75
00:06:18,793 --> 00:06:20,875
[SPEAKER_01]: So, normally, that sentence just sort of gets... Sure.

76
00:06:20,895 --> 00:06:24,917
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, there are many, like, many examples in the suttas exactly that.

77
00:06:25,437 --> 00:06:29,960
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it's not... like, the common translations are not wrong, but they're clearly not accurate enough.

78
00:06:30,100 --> 00:06:30,340
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

79
00:06:30,641 --> 00:06:32,602
[SPEAKER_00]: Or they clearly don't reveal

80
00:06:33,545 --> 00:06:40,167
[SPEAKER_00]: The the precise emphasis on certain things, but it's more like a generic, oh, dhamma is great, dhamma is good, and dhamma is steadfast.

81
00:06:41,068 --> 00:06:45,109
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, yes, but how can knowledge of that result in direct arahantship?

82
00:06:45,269 --> 00:06:45,509
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

83
00:06:47,130 --> 00:06:48,110
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you realize it can't.

84
00:06:48,391 --> 00:06:51,092
[SPEAKER_00]: There's one example I was talking before, was the

85
00:06:52,769 --> 00:06:54,070
[SPEAKER_00]: Feeling prey to suffering.

86
00:06:54,510 --> 00:06:56,912
[SPEAKER_00]: The young man leaves and says, oh, yeah, yeah, yeah, sure, sure.

87
00:06:56,932 --> 00:06:57,333
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no.

88
00:06:57,753 --> 00:06:58,814
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop and think about that.

89
00:06:58,954 --> 00:06:59,554
[SPEAKER_00]: What does that mean?

90
00:07:00,415 --> 00:07:06,700
[SPEAKER_00]: Feeling prey to suffering means being subject to, like, feeling like you're being hunted, but you don't know who the hunter is.

91
00:07:06,720 --> 00:07:08,061
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you are actually being hunted.

92
00:07:08,221 --> 00:07:09,202
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you're not being hunted.

93
00:07:09,322 --> 00:07:09,962
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm prey.

94
00:07:10,443 --> 00:07:13,565
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm in this jungle, and I feel like I'm preying here.

95
00:07:14,150 --> 00:07:17,331
[SPEAKER_00]: And I don't know by whom, from whom, why, from which direction.

96
00:07:17,871 --> 00:07:19,052
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how would you then feel?

97
00:07:19,092 --> 00:07:20,973
[SPEAKER_00]: How would you describe the feeling of a prey?

98
00:07:21,033 --> 00:07:27,315
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, anxiety, dread, guilt, or can I do something about it, I'm not doing anything about this, all these things.

99
00:07:27,355 --> 00:07:35,158
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like, oh, that's the existential situation that needs to precede the right type of search towards freedom from suffering, the right type of practice.

100
00:07:36,058 --> 00:07:37,579
[SPEAKER_00]: And yet, everybody would just read over it.

101
00:07:38,039 --> 00:07:39,079
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, pray for suffering, yeah.

102
00:07:39,279 --> 00:07:46,943
[SPEAKER_00]: How many people actually allow themselves to feel pray to saṃsāra, pray to existence, pray to elements, pray to the body, pray to sickness, aging and death?

103
00:07:47,703 --> 00:07:48,063
[SPEAKER_00]: Not many.

104
00:07:48,884 --> 00:07:51,745
[SPEAKER_00]: Even the ones that practice the dhamma don't start on that basis.

105
00:07:51,885 --> 00:07:54,146
[SPEAKER_00]: They allow it maybe to some degree, but not fully.

106
00:07:57,047 --> 00:08:02,990
[SPEAKER_01]: Sort of explore what this... what it... what this kind of strange phrase means.

107
00:08:03,230 --> 00:08:03,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

108
00:08:03,670 --> 00:08:03,970
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

109
00:08:05,398 --> 00:08:20,263
[SPEAKER_01]: It's interesting, he says, beforehand, su-summa, dhamma titiññāna, dhamma titiññāna, so, the knowledge of the endurance of things, afterwards, the knowledge of nibbāna.

110
00:08:21,684 --> 00:08:33,828
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, the reason why, why your translation is probably more correct, is because it also ties in with other practices that we have talked about that result in nibbāna.

111
00:08:34,522 --> 00:08:55,965
[SPEAKER_00]: Like the the Buddha Vipassi, that when he discerned the paticca upādāna, as in the principle, and then he remained mindful of the endurance of the five aggregates, the buddhahoodism, and then his mind became completely purified and liberated.

112
00:08:58,110 --> 00:09:06,320
[SPEAKER_00]: And you realize that, for most people, recognizing these three characteristics, arising is manifest, ceasing is manifest, persisting or changing is manifest.

113
00:09:07,201 --> 00:09:10,084
[SPEAKER_00]: If you understand that, you'll be on the level of the Sotāpanna, probably.

114
00:09:10,485 --> 00:09:14,810
[SPEAKER_00]: Because today's contemporary wrong views are so much rooted in that.

115
00:09:15,970 --> 00:09:29,377
[SPEAKER_00]: The kind of the perception of time, the gratuitous perception of time, the projection of the external world, self self-view outside of yourself and all that, and all of that would be uprooted if you were to understand that phenomena has arisen and has the nature to persist on its own.

116
00:09:29,838 --> 00:09:30,078
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

117
00:09:30,198 --> 00:09:31,238
[SPEAKER_00]: They have nothing to do with you.

118
00:09:31,679 --> 00:09:37,121
[SPEAKER_00]: You and your reactions and your mental movements in regard to the arisen phenomena are secondary to the phenomena.

119
00:09:37,582 --> 00:09:39,503
[SPEAKER_00]: Simultaneous, but secondary.

120
00:09:39,883 --> 00:09:43,325
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's... so the first... so this dhamma, cittanyāna,

121
00:09:44,009 --> 00:09:52,373
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like this, just recognizing that things have arisen, they've already arisen and they are enduring.

122
00:09:52,433 --> 00:09:57,856
[SPEAKER_00]: It means like knowledge, pretty much the full understanding of the nature of an enduring phenomenon, dhamma.

123
00:09:58,276 --> 00:09:58,476
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

124
00:09:59,877 --> 00:10:03,879
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you have full understanding of that, as I said, this passion will be a natural result.

125
00:10:03,899 --> 00:10:08,922
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's, it's interesting that it's, so it's different, it's being distinguished from the knowledge of nibbāna.

126
00:10:09,722 --> 00:10:10,863
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, you precede it.

127
00:10:10,983 --> 00:10:11,183
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

128
00:10:11,512 --> 00:10:16,555
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's something you would... upon which the dispassion, which is what nibbāna is, can be developed.

129
00:10:16,675 --> 00:10:16,895
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

130
00:10:18,696 --> 00:10:33,123
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, I... Basically, you can say that's the knowledge of nibbāna, but in order for you to be nibbāna, you have to stay with that knowledge sufficient amount of time until your whole experience has became, like, cooled down, dispassionate, and then you are in nibbāna.

131
00:10:33,363 --> 00:10:33,963
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like...

132
00:10:33,983 --> 00:10:39,846
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the first is about the endurance of things, and the second is about the cessation of things.

133
00:10:40,870 --> 00:10:44,152
[SPEAKER_01]: The cessation of things' existence, of things being, of that chance...

134
00:10:44,172 --> 00:11:00,279
[SPEAKER_00]: The cessation of their endurance, the... Because, like... and the reason why endurance is singled out there, is because arising is a form of endurance, ceasing is a form of endurance, resisting or changing is a form of endurance.

135
00:11:00,359 --> 00:11:00,699
[SPEAKER_00]: It's manifested.

136
00:11:00,839 --> 00:11:01,239
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

137
00:11:01,819 --> 00:11:07,942
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why Buddha says, well, the first mistake when people come across this is they misread it.

138
00:11:09,326 --> 00:11:17,087
[SPEAKER_00]: It always says, arising is manifest, ceasing is manifested, persisting or changing, changing while standing, depending on translation, is manifested.

139
00:11:17,940 --> 00:11:22,944
[SPEAKER_00]: The three periods, the three... sorry, the three periods, past, present, future, but then this is manifested within those periods.

140
00:11:23,805 --> 00:11:31,031
[SPEAKER_00]: But people will always read that as arising is manifested, persisting while changing is manifested, ceasing is manifested.

141
00:11:31,051 --> 00:11:31,812
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

142
00:11:31,952 --> 00:11:36,976
[SPEAKER_00]: It's assumed to be like this linear, basically, just observance of a duration of a thing.

143
00:11:37,657 --> 00:11:38,157
[SPEAKER_00]: But it isn't.

144
00:11:38,457 --> 00:11:39,318
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha didn't say that.

145
00:11:39,358 --> 00:11:41,340
[SPEAKER_00]: He said arising and ceasing are manifested.

146
00:11:41,956 --> 00:11:43,700
[SPEAKER_00]: And persisting while changing is manifesting.

147
00:11:43,720 --> 00:11:44,362
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that?

148
00:11:44,883 --> 00:11:45,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's not linear.

149
00:11:46,768 --> 00:11:48,812
[SPEAKER_00]: And you realize, okay, let's look at the arising.

150
00:11:48,953 --> 00:11:49,695
[SPEAKER_00]: What is arising?

151
00:11:51,622 --> 00:11:54,324
[SPEAKER_00]: Arising is basically increasing in its presence of something.

152
00:11:54,624 --> 00:11:55,565
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, this is arising.

153
00:11:56,386 --> 00:11:58,788
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha doesn't say, oh, arising starts.

154
00:11:58,968 --> 00:11:59,088
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

155
00:11:59,168 --> 00:12:01,150
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, arising is manifested.

156
00:12:01,170 --> 00:12:01,650
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

157
00:12:01,750 --> 00:12:03,231
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like you see...

158
00:12:03,612 --> 00:12:09,677
[SPEAKER_00]: The point when you just find what's manifested, you see it as already arising.

159
00:12:10,117 --> 00:12:10,717
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, same.

160
00:12:10,817 --> 00:12:12,519
[SPEAKER_00]: Ceasing is manifested.

161
00:12:12,999 --> 00:12:16,762
[SPEAKER_00]: Not ceasing has ceased and there is the end point after which... No.

162
00:12:16,862 --> 00:12:18,564
[SPEAKER_00]: Ceasing is manifested.

163
00:12:19,124 --> 00:12:21,885
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means ceasing is another arisen experience for you.

164
00:12:22,446 --> 00:12:28,228
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, increasing in its presence is arising, decreasing in its presence is ceasing, but it's still there.

165
00:12:28,749 --> 00:12:35,312
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, that phenomena is persisting while changing, or changing while remaining the same.

166
00:12:36,032 --> 00:12:46,777
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if I say, I'm having, like, a good mood, like, I become aware, my good mood is manifested, and you could see it either arising or either ceasing.

167
00:12:47,546 --> 00:12:49,187
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not gonna... there's no third option to it.

168
00:12:49,868 --> 00:12:57,074
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that manifestation of a good mood that you just recognized, you became mindful of, you could see there's... oh, it's increasing in its presence.

169
00:12:57,974 --> 00:13:00,496
[SPEAKER_00]: It's lasting to words, so to speak.

170
00:13:01,317 --> 00:13:02,758
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you not... can you not...

171
00:13:05,200 --> 00:13:08,743
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't see it as particularly... it seems to be just...

172
00:13:09,885 --> 00:13:10,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no.

173
00:13:10,946 --> 00:13:11,907
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the point.

174
00:13:11,947 --> 00:13:13,868
[SPEAKER_00]: Manifestation means it's there.

175
00:13:13,908 --> 00:13:15,650
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what you refer to when you say it's steady.

176
00:13:16,050 --> 00:13:20,574
[SPEAKER_00]: Within that, you could see it's either towards me or away from me, so to speak.

177
00:13:20,854 --> 00:13:22,696
[SPEAKER_00]: Towards my point of view or away from it.

178
00:13:24,151 --> 00:13:30,196
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you look at it closely, you realize, oh, yeah, it's kind of there with a kind of prospect of being even more of itself.

179
00:13:30,896 --> 00:13:32,758
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's arising.

180
00:13:32,818 --> 00:13:33,459
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what it means.

181
00:13:33,479 --> 00:13:38,002
[SPEAKER_00]: It's increasing in its presence, while it's still the same mood that's been manifested.

182
00:13:38,663 --> 00:13:43,807
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why I keep saying to people, practically, if you want to reflect on these principles, you can't do it.

183
00:13:44,127 --> 00:13:47,390
[SPEAKER_00]: Arising is manifest, duration, ceasing, okay, next.

184
00:13:47,870 --> 00:13:48,371
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you're not.

185
00:13:48,411 --> 00:13:49,411
[SPEAKER_00]: You're leaving yourself out.

186
00:13:50,439 --> 00:13:51,980
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the manifestation is already there.

187
00:13:52,100 --> 00:13:53,060
[SPEAKER_00]: Manifestation of what?

188
00:13:53,440 --> 00:13:56,081
[SPEAKER_00]: Of something arising for you or something seizing away from you.

189
00:13:56,521 --> 00:14:00,742
[SPEAKER_00]: As in my good mood, I can feel it sort of fading, basically.

190
00:14:01,043 --> 00:14:02,303
[SPEAKER_00]: Fading in and fading out.

191
00:14:03,883 --> 00:14:05,344
[SPEAKER_00]: The phenomena are always directional.

192
00:14:06,426 --> 00:14:12,148
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can no fading in or fading out on account of it being there already and remaining the same thing.

193
00:14:12,468 --> 00:14:20,451
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there would be no fading in, for example, nor fading out, if a thing changes from the point A to the point B to the point C. No, it remains the same thing.

194
00:14:20,471 --> 00:14:24,092
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you can say it's increasing of the same.

195
00:14:24,712 --> 00:14:29,514
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's a completely different thing, there would be no connection of arising or ceasing for that matter.

196
00:14:30,114 --> 00:14:33,696
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why you have arising and ceasing that are manifested are on one level.

197
00:14:34,497 --> 00:14:37,598
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, their nature is to persist while changing.

198
00:14:37,618 --> 00:14:39,099
[SPEAKER_00]: And recognizing that nature...

199
00:14:39,279 --> 00:14:48,544
[SPEAKER_00]: If you recognize the nature of enduring, persisting while changing, or changing while remaining the same, you're already recognizing, discerning arising and ceasing of things.

200
00:14:48,785 --> 00:14:49,725
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is what the Buddha said.

201
00:14:49,765 --> 00:14:54,708
[SPEAKER_00]: He discerned, for Buddha Vipassana, he discerned arising or ceasing of the five aggregates.

202
00:14:54,788 --> 00:14:55,248
[SPEAKER_00]: And so this is...

203
00:14:55,268 --> 00:14:56,549
[SPEAKER_01]: He discerned their endurance.

204
00:14:56,589 --> 00:14:58,210
[SPEAKER_01]: This is what dhamma, cittanyana is.

205
00:14:58,670 --> 00:15:01,732
[SPEAKER_00]: That kind of knowledge has included everything else we did.

206
00:15:01,992 --> 00:15:14,701
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you develop the knowledge of enduring phenomena, means you develop the knowledge of non-interfering with gratuitous assumptions with these phenomena, which also then means you develop the knowledge of impossibility to own these phenomena.

207
00:15:15,141 --> 00:15:18,463
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is no sense of ownership maintained in regard to phenomena.

208
00:15:18,543 --> 00:15:21,285
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no sense of self that's maintained as a result of that ownership.

209
00:15:22,326 --> 00:15:25,448
[SPEAKER_00]: So, nibbāna is the natural product of that kind of understanding.

210
00:15:25,628 --> 00:15:25,888
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

211
00:15:27,320 --> 00:15:50,309
[SPEAKER_01]: Jnāna-vīra says at one point that seeing the three characteristics isn't isn't necessarily seeing anicca, doesn't doesn't doesn't isn't only seeing anicca.

212
00:15:51,449 --> 00:15:52,910
[SPEAKER_01]: So Venerable Jnana-vīra says

213
00:15:55,799 --> 00:16:07,374
[SPEAKER_01]: Talking about these three sankhātās, the sankhātālakanāyam, so the characteristics, the determined characteristics of... Yeah, the characteristics of the determined, yeah.

214
00:16:07,555 --> 00:16:09,497
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what it is to be a thing, basically.

215
00:16:09,517 --> 00:16:09,638
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

216
00:16:10,423 --> 00:16:13,085
[SPEAKER_00]: ...is that you are basically manifested on your own.

217
00:16:13,105 --> 00:16:21,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Within a manifestation, you discern either it's increasing in its presence of the same manifestation, or it's decreasing in its presence of the same manifestation, remaining the same manifestation.

218
00:16:21,870 --> 00:16:24,812
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, it's another manifestation that's either increasing or decreasing.

219
00:16:24,832 --> 00:16:28,434
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no linear increasing immediately leads to decreasing that leads to the new thing.

220
00:16:28,454 --> 00:16:28,634
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

221
00:16:28,754 --> 00:16:32,657
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a completely external assumption of how the world is for you.

222
00:16:32,797 --> 00:16:36,980
[SPEAKER_00]: But from your point of view, all you have is feeling, intention, perceptions.

223
00:16:37,520 --> 00:16:38,781
[SPEAKER_00]: They're there, enduring.

224
00:16:39,501 --> 00:16:42,743
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes enduring more towards you, sometimes enduring away from you.

225
00:16:43,583 --> 00:16:50,526
[SPEAKER_00]: But the only reason you can... towards, or away, or increasing, or decreasing, can be intelligible, is because the same thing remains.

226
00:16:50,546 --> 00:16:51,807
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, something is there.

227
00:16:52,127 --> 00:16:58,249
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why, Puṭṭhujana, it's probably the hardest thing for him to understand, is that, allowing phenomena to endure on its own.

228
00:16:58,269 --> 00:17:01,571
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can have the same feeling for five days.

229
00:17:02,399 --> 00:17:03,101
[SPEAKER_00]: The same mood.

230
00:17:03,541 --> 00:17:04,663
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's still the same mood.

231
00:17:05,304 --> 00:17:06,626
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, you wouldn't have a memory of it.

232
00:17:07,507 --> 00:17:08,348
[SPEAKER_00]: You couldn't remember it.

233
00:17:09,070 --> 00:17:10,892
[SPEAKER_00]: And you've been feeling like this for a number of days.

234
00:17:11,960 --> 00:17:26,703
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's this completely kind of fabricated assumption of the external world as the true objective world that I can access with my subjectivity objectively, which is already a contradiction in terms, and then all I have to do is observe that world, and that's how I improve my sense of self.

235
00:17:26,783 --> 00:17:29,804
[SPEAKER_00]: Failing to see that you'll always remain within your sense of self whether you want it or not.

236
00:17:29,864 --> 00:17:37,065
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you think about a public world, that world is part of your experience, and as such, it's unpublic, which means your thinking is a contradiction in terms.

237
00:17:42,099 --> 00:17:46,821
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what leads people to doing these, like, observational, methodical meditation techniques and practices.

238
00:17:47,301 --> 00:17:50,842
[SPEAKER_00]: Whereby, I'm feeling this, I'm feeling this, I'm feeling this, oh, I'm feeling this, I'm feeling...

239
00:17:50,882 --> 00:17:53,503
[SPEAKER_00]: Completely unaware of a feeling enduring on its own.

240
00:17:54,064 --> 00:17:55,364
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to be attending to it.

241
00:17:55,384 --> 00:17:56,965
[SPEAKER_00]: It's there whether you want it or not.

242
00:17:57,465 --> 00:17:58,805
[SPEAKER_00]: And same goes for the five aggregates.

243
00:17:59,386 --> 00:17:59,946
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what we said.

244
00:18:00,306 --> 00:18:02,087
[SPEAKER_00]: Upādāna is not in the five aggregates.

245
00:18:02,147 --> 00:18:05,608
[SPEAKER_00]: Cannot be in the... cannot be part of the structure of your experience.

246
00:18:07,313 --> 00:18:13,585
[SPEAKER_00]: It's desire and lust, wrong views in regard to the structure of your experience, in regard to the five aggregates, upādāna is there.

247
00:18:14,286 --> 00:18:19,816
[SPEAKER_01]: It's almost like people doing these practices are trying to make something endure.

248
00:18:20,408 --> 00:18:20,928
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

249
00:18:21,308 --> 00:18:22,028
[SPEAKER_00]: As if it's up to you.

250
00:18:22,108 --> 00:18:23,229
[SPEAKER_00]: As if it's up to you, yeah.

251
00:18:23,289 --> 00:18:25,169
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why that mindfulness is not really mindfulness.

252
00:18:25,849 --> 00:18:28,170
[SPEAKER_00]: It's something... because mindfulness is done properly, it's effortless.

253
00:18:28,330 --> 00:18:30,630
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you can't have too much of it, as the Buddha said himself.

254
00:18:31,910 --> 00:18:40,492
[SPEAKER_00]: It is effortless, because it's based on discerning the enduring presence of the body, of the feelings, of the thoughts, and the mind.

255
00:18:41,292 --> 00:18:42,012
[SPEAKER_00]: Endure on their own.

256
00:18:43,713 --> 00:18:44,413
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can only

257
00:18:45,207 --> 00:18:48,870
[SPEAKER_01]: See that by just starting to... To recognize what endurance is.

258
00:18:49,050 --> 00:18:50,631
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's already there.

259
00:18:51,111 --> 00:18:54,894
[SPEAKER_00]: By recognizing what endurance is, you get skilled in discerning all three characteristics.

260
00:18:55,675 --> 00:19:04,421
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why Buddha said some monks are skilled in discerning arising, some monks are skilled in discerning ceasing, some monks are skilled in discerning, like, enduring.

261
00:19:05,241 --> 00:19:05,582
[SPEAKER_00]: Or all.

262
00:19:05,982 --> 00:19:10,305
[SPEAKER_00]: But the point is, if you're discerning arising correctly, you're already discerning enduring.

263
00:19:11,206 --> 00:19:14,248
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're discerning ceasing correctly, you're already discerning enduring.

264
00:19:15,050 --> 00:19:19,154
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're discerning and doing correctly, you're already discerning all of it correctly.

265
00:19:19,794 --> 00:19:24,659
[SPEAKER_00]: So, either of those things can serve as a basis for full understanding of that principle that leads to dispassion.

266
00:19:26,160 --> 00:19:27,862
[SPEAKER_01]: But, so that... but that's the interesting thing.

267
00:19:27,902 --> 00:19:34,208
[SPEAKER_01]: So, these three characteristics concisely indicate the fundamental structure in virtue of which things are things.

268
00:19:35,306 --> 00:19:35,486
[SPEAKER_01]: Mine.

269
00:19:35,526 --> 00:19:35,646
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

270
00:19:55,148 --> 00:20:04,581
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you recognize endurance of a recent phenomenon to the full extent, as I said, you will be unable to maintain your sense of ownership of it, because you realize it's completely independent.

271
00:20:04,941 --> 00:20:07,305
[SPEAKER_00]: It's completely emotionally indifferent to you.

272
00:20:08,698 --> 00:20:10,439
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, it doesn't even know you exist.

273
00:20:10,920 --> 00:20:13,362
[SPEAKER_00]: You... your sense of self is redundant to it.

274
00:20:13,962 --> 00:20:18,405
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's like it's... it's a fundamental structure that applies to both the pūtūjana and the arahant.

275
00:20:18,565 --> 00:20:18,826
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no.

276
00:20:18,886 --> 00:20:19,606
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the five aggregates.

277
00:20:19,626 --> 00:20:21,027
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the fundamental structure, yes.

278
00:20:21,127 --> 00:20:21,307
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

279
00:20:21,408 --> 00:20:23,789
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no hidden structure underneath that, because that's all you have.

280
00:20:23,809 --> 00:20:24,410
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

281
00:20:24,430 --> 00:20:25,010
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what I'm saying.

282
00:20:25,030 --> 00:20:31,835
[SPEAKER_00]: The only difference, then, between somebody who fully understood that and somebody who hasn't, is passion and dispassion, which is pūtūjana and arahant.

283
00:20:31,875 --> 00:20:32,055
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

284
00:20:33,356 --> 00:20:33,737
[SPEAKER_00]: Nibbāna

285
00:20:38,888 --> 00:20:43,813
[SPEAKER_00]: So, cooling down, removal of lust, the fires of greed and aversion, and so on.

286
00:20:43,973 --> 00:20:51,962
[SPEAKER_01]: The point I want to make here is that Paramahānāvīrā goes on to say that the question of impermanence is not simply that of establishing these three characteristics.

287
00:20:53,343 --> 00:20:56,667
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no, not simply establishing characteristics in the sense of what Tujana does.

288
00:20:57,347 --> 00:20:59,169
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, look, lusting and disappearing.

289
00:21:00,066 --> 00:21:01,126
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what impermanence is.

290
00:21:01,246 --> 00:21:01,526
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

291
00:21:01,546 --> 00:21:05,427
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you'd say that seeing the three characteristics in the right way... Yeah, that's why... Sure.

292
00:21:05,447 --> 00:21:09,588
[SPEAKER_00]: Because anicca is that impossibility to own the thing.

293
00:21:09,908 --> 00:21:13,369
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like, look, thing has disappeared, that's why it's impermanent.

294
00:21:13,729 --> 00:21:14,510
[SPEAKER_00]: That doesn't make sense.

295
00:21:15,030 --> 00:21:15,390
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

296
00:21:15,410 --> 00:21:17,630
[SPEAKER_00]: Because disappearance is manifested.

297
00:21:17,750 --> 00:21:22,812
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, you're looking at the content of what has disappeared or rather changed its appearance, pretty much.

298
00:21:24,852 --> 00:21:27,513
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, claim that to be some fundamental anicca.

299
00:21:28,578 --> 00:21:30,499
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you then do that for your five aggregates?

300
00:21:30,519 --> 00:21:35,762
[SPEAKER_00]: You will need to destroy your five aggregates, kill them off, in order to see them as impermanent.

301
00:21:36,243 --> 00:21:39,064
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means then all you need to do is die to become an arahant.

302
00:21:39,945 --> 00:21:41,086
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not the case, isn't it?

303
00:21:41,666 --> 00:21:43,427
[SPEAKER_00]: The five aggregates are not affected by death.

304
00:21:44,468 --> 00:21:47,069
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence the rebirth, hence the perpetuation of saṃsāra.

305
00:21:47,510 --> 00:21:50,211
[SPEAKER_00]: And if even you remember your previous lives, you remember the five aggregates.

306
00:21:50,291 --> 00:21:50,992
[SPEAKER_00]: They do remain.

307
00:21:52,871 --> 00:21:55,593
[SPEAKER_00]: And when you say, oh, so the five aggregates move from this life to the next life.

308
00:21:55,653 --> 00:21:55,813
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

309
00:21:56,334 --> 00:22:00,357
[SPEAKER_00]: This life and the next life are already within the five aggregates, so nothing moves.

310
00:22:01,197 --> 00:22:02,698
[SPEAKER_00]: Just the content of it changes.

311
00:22:04,740 --> 00:22:07,562
[SPEAKER_00]: For all of us who wonder, how is rebirth possible?

312
00:22:11,964 --> 00:22:13,766
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just... it's interesting.

313
00:22:13,786 --> 00:22:19,972
[SPEAKER_01]: He just seems to be saying that the question of impermanence is not simply that of establishing these three characteristics.

314
00:22:20,052 --> 00:22:24,856
[SPEAKER_01]: It's almost like these three characteristics that things endure.

315
00:22:27,238 --> 00:22:34,065
[SPEAKER_01]: And the knowledge of the endurance of... the endurance of that knowledge, which is also a thing, which is enduring.

316
00:22:35,931 --> 00:22:42,916
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, in a way, then, in itself, you could argue it's not enough, as in, you need a bit more instruction to know how to regard things as not mine.

317
00:22:43,256 --> 00:22:47,639
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's like, even that can be taken away.

318
00:22:48,740 --> 00:22:52,122
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no good reason why that... Yeah, sure.

319
00:22:52,342 --> 00:22:58,487
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's something like... Well, I'm pretty certain that all those teachers with the wrong views in the suttas,

320
00:22:59,315 --> 00:23:03,656
[SPEAKER_00]: These things are actually quite right, from the point of view of modern wrong views.

321
00:23:04,736 --> 00:23:13,899
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when the teacher was teaching the basis of neither perception nor perception, yeah, it's not arhantic, but how will you arrive there without being fully aware of enduring phenomenon of mind?

322
00:23:14,579 --> 00:23:25,842
[SPEAKER_00]: Different extents of the mind that endure, which means you can be fully skilled in discerning these things, but it's that passion, this passion side,

323
00:23:26,768 --> 00:23:29,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you need to... you need to understand.

324
00:23:29,689 --> 00:23:45,911
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you understand these things rightly, as in how the Buddha taught, in a sense of, because of this, it can't be mine, and start kind of applying it back onto yourself, in terms of not entertaining the retreater's sense of ownership, that will result in dispassion.

325
00:23:46,251 --> 00:23:50,872
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, that's what Buddha implies with proper understanding of enduring phenomena, proper understanding of dhamma.

326
00:23:51,372 --> 00:23:54,973
[SPEAKER_00]: I think, in the Vedas, he talks about it in like a structural sense, which is also accurate,

327
00:23:55,543 --> 00:24:01,507
[SPEAKER_00]: But in itself, does not necessarily mean you will be regarding it as not mine.

328
00:24:01,767 --> 00:24:12,754
[SPEAKER_01]: But the Buddha is also making this distinction between the knowledge of the endurance of things, these three characteristics, or the third characteristic, which... Yeah, the knowledge that... No, it's not the same distinction.

329
00:24:12,814 --> 00:24:14,235
[SPEAKER_00]: He's making distinction...

330
00:24:14,255 --> 00:24:20,999
[SPEAKER_00]: There is a distinction between when Yajurā says, outlining these three characteristics, and when the Buddha says, knowledge of the three characteristics.

331
00:24:21,019 --> 00:24:22,120
[SPEAKER_00]: Knowledge of the three characteristics, okay.

332
00:24:22,140 --> 00:24:23,461
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, I see what you mean.

333
00:24:23,481 --> 00:24:24,001
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

334
00:24:24,655 --> 00:24:28,617
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he'd be saying, so the... so beforehand, there's the knowledge of the endurance of things.

335
00:24:28,837 --> 00:24:30,497
[SPEAKER_01]: Afterwards, there's the knowledge of nibbāna.

336
00:24:30,898 --> 00:24:31,118
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

337
00:24:31,138 --> 00:24:34,459
[SPEAKER_00]: As if they are... You become familiar with this passion that you now fully understand.

338
00:24:35,359 --> 00:24:36,620
[SPEAKER_01]: But is that not also like...

339
00:24:38,881 --> 00:24:43,643
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the knowledge of nibbāna means not... means knowledge of...

340
00:24:45,040 --> 00:24:49,722
[SPEAKER_01]: The possibility of the cessation of even things enduring, anything enduring.

341
00:24:50,022 --> 00:24:50,182
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

342
00:24:50,282 --> 00:24:53,963
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's... that knowledge already starts with knowledge of the things.

343
00:24:54,403 --> 00:24:54,603
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

344
00:24:55,044 --> 00:24:55,244
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

345
00:24:55,964 --> 00:25:03,947
[SPEAKER_00]: If properly understood, as Buddha Vipassana did, he got the knowledge of principle of pratīcā-upādā, as in, determined by something that cannot be mine.

346
00:25:04,750 --> 00:25:11,057
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, he remained basically contemplating the five aggregates against the backdrop of that insight.

347
00:25:11,338 --> 00:25:11,558
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

348
00:25:11,938 --> 00:25:13,000
[SPEAKER_00]: And he became an arahant.

349
00:25:13,440 --> 00:25:14,181
[SPEAKER_00]: This is quite interesting.

350
00:25:14,221 --> 00:25:15,002
[SPEAKER_01]: It's temporal.

351
00:25:16,228 --> 00:25:17,369
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a thing going on here.

352
00:25:17,469 --> 00:25:20,711
[SPEAKER_01]: It makes me think of... Well, it's not temporal.

353
00:25:21,071 --> 00:25:22,913
[SPEAKER_00]: Neither of those knowledges... No, no, no.

354
00:25:22,973 --> 00:25:23,693
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't mean that.

355
00:25:23,713 --> 00:25:25,154
[SPEAKER_00]: It's only that you need to...

356
00:25:25,234 --> 00:25:32,359
[SPEAKER_00]: It takes time for all the passion to sort of dissipate from all sorts of aspects that you've been cultivating for a lifetime.

357
00:25:32,800 --> 00:25:38,744
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in that sense, it takes time, but it's basically persistence of the same right view, which is timeless, because of that.

358
00:25:38,804 --> 00:25:39,885
[SPEAKER_00]: It persists through all this time.

359
00:25:40,025 --> 00:25:42,687
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't change through it, through that time.

360
00:25:43,765 --> 00:25:45,026
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you see it as well.

361
00:25:45,046 --> 00:25:46,226
[SPEAKER_01]: I think it's the same thing.

362
00:25:46,306 --> 00:25:50,028
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I... so, the... the...

363
00:25:50,949 --> 00:25:51,929
[SPEAKER_01]: I think it's somewhere in the...

364
00:25:51,949 --> 00:26:12,740
[SPEAKER_01]: I think it's in the Udāna, where the... it is... it's the Udāna, where the Buddha says, in the... after his enlightenment, when he first became the Buddha, he's sitting under the Bodhi tree, in Bodhgaya, and in the first watch of the night,

